<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<databaseChangeLog
    xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
    xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
    xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog
        http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-4.29.xsd">

    <!--
      Program C. `nlq.settings.manage` — may this person set the restaurant's own AI provider and
      API key.

      ══ What the screen is ══

      /app/settings/ai lets a tenant supply their OWN AI API key. Until now nlq-service read ONE
      fleet-wide key from deploy config (`restaurantos.nlq.anthropic.api-key`), so every tenant's
      NLQ traffic billed to a single Anthropic account with no per-tenant attribution, quota or
      isolation. That is a multi-tenancy defect, not merely an absent feature.

      ══ Why this is NOT nlq.query.run ══

      `nlq.query.run` (changeset 046) is "ask a question", held by OWNER, TENANT_ADMIN, MANAGER and
      ACCOUNTANT. This is "hand the platform a credential that bills to our account, and decide
      whose account every question in this restaurant is charged to from now on". They share a
      noun and nothing else — the precedent for splitting on exactly that is 091 (pos.tax.manage),
      093 (pos.service_charge.manage) and 19b (pos.tables.admin).

      ══ Why a PERMISSION and not a role check ══

      An earlier attempt at this feature (origin/Mufazzal @ d11d4ae5) guarded its endpoints with
      @PreAuthorize("hasAnyAuthority('ROLE_OWNER','ROLE_TENANT_ADMIN')"). Shared-lib's
      JwtAuthenticationFilter builds authorities from the raw `roles` and `permissions` claims with
      SimpleGrantedAuthority::new and NEVER adds a ROLE_ prefix, so a real owner's token carries
      OWNER, never ROLE_OWNER — and hasAnyAuthority is an exact string match. Every endpoint on
      that branch 403s the owner it was built for: a complete settings surface that reads correctly
      and cannot run.

      A permission also lets changeset 092's tenant custom roles be granted this; a hard-coded role
      list never can.

      ══ Who holds it ══

      OWNER and TENANT_ADMIN only — the same two personas /app/settings already admits, and the
      same holder set 089, 090, 091 and 093 chose.

      MANAGER is deliberately excluded, including from the READ. Unlike the service charge (093),
      where a manager must be able to look up a number printed on every guest's bill, there is
      nothing here a manager needs to explain to anyone: the screen shows a provider, a key state,
      and the last four characters of a credential. Excluding them keeps the masked hint and the
      billing posture inside the two roles that own the commercial relationship.

      This widens nothing: before this changeset no role could set an AI key, because no endpoint
      existed that could.

      ══ Why the grant is explicit ══

      Same reason 055, 059, 082, 083, 085, 088, 089, 090, 091 and 093 each give: changeset 036's
      blanket "grant every permission to OWNER / TENANT_ADMIN" statements are runOnChange="false"
      and ran ONCE at initial database creation. They do NOT retroactively pick up a permission
      added later. A permission that no role holds does not fail at startup, does not break a test,
      and produces a clean confident 403 for every user including OWNER — indistinguishable from a
      deliberate denial. Hence the verification changeSet below, which RAISEs rather than warns.
    -->

    <changeSet id="auth-1.0.0-095-nlq-ai-settings-manage" author="restaurantos-agent" runOnChange="false">
        <comment>nlq.settings.manage — set the tenant's own AI provider and API key; held by OWNER and TENANT_ADMIN</comment>
        <sql splitStatements="true" endDelimiter=";">
            INSERT INTO permissions (code, module, description) VALUES
              ('nlq.settings.manage', 'nlq', 'Set this restaurant''s own AI provider and API key for natural-language queries')
            ON CONFLICT (code) DO NOTHING;

            INSERT INTO role_permissions (role_code, permission_code) VALUES
              ('OWNER',        'nlq.settings.manage'),
              ('TENANT_ADMIN', 'nlq.settings.manage')
            ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
        </sql>

        <rollback>
            <sql splitStatements="true" endDelimiter=";">
                DELETE FROM role_permissions WHERE permission_code = 'nlq.settings.manage';
                DELETE FROM permissions WHERE code = 'nlq.settings.manage';
            </sql>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

    <changeSet id="auth-1.0.0-095-verify-nlq-ai-settings-manage" author="restaurantos-agent" runOnChange="false">
        <comment>Fail the migration if nlq.settings.manage exists but no role holds it</comment>
        <sql splitStatements="false">
            DO $$
            DECLARE holder_count INT;
            BEGIN
                SELECT count(*) INTO holder_count
                  FROM role_permissions
                 WHERE permission_code = 'nlq.settings.manage';
                IF holder_count = 0 THEN
                    RAISE EXCEPTION
                      'nlq.settings.manage is defined but granted to no role. nlq-service refuses every call to /api/v1/nlq/settings/ai without it, so the Settings -> AI screen would render an access refusal for every user on every tenant including OWNER - as an ordinary-looking permission denial, not an error anyone would trace back to this migration.';
                END IF;
            END $$;
        </sql>
        <rollback/>
    </changeSet>

</databaseChangeLog>
